<% content_for :title, 'Planetary body' %>

<%= render 'help/page', title: 'Planetary Body' do %>
  <div class='space-y-6'>
      <div class='space-y-3  text-fg'>
        <p>
          Applies to terrestrial planets, planetoids, and moons. Sections are shown only when
          data is present. Orbital Data is omitted for rogue bodies not attached to a star.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class=' font-semibold text-highlight mb-2'>Controls</p>
        <dl class='space-y-5'>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Regenerate</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Available on terrestrial planets and planetoid belts that orbit a stellar primary.
              Submits the body's Universal World Profile and orbital parameters to the generator
              data core, which recomputes and returns a complete characteristic profile.
              All existing survey data for the body is superseded by the returned values.
            </dd>
            <dd class='mt-2 text-fg'>
              For terrestrial planets, the satellite inventory is also replaced — all previously
              recorded moons are expunged and reconstructed from the generator's output. For
              planetoid belts, the significant body catalogue is similarly replaced. This
              operation is irreversible.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class=' font-semibold text-highlight mb-2'>Header</p>
        <dl class='space-y-5 '>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>UWP</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Universal World Profile — a seven-digit hex code encoding starport, size, atmosphere, hydrographics, population, government, and law level.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Starport</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Class of the body's primary port facility, from A (excellent) through E (frontier) and X (none).</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Berthing Cost</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Rolled per the starport class on landing (WBH p. 257): 1D×Cr1000 for Class A, 1D×Cr500
              for Class B, 1D×Cr100 for Class C, 1D×Cr10 for Class D. Shown as a dash for Class E and X,
              which have no berthing service.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Fuel Cost</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Shown as two unlabelled figures, in order: refined fuel cost first, then
              unrefined fuel cost. Refined fuel costs Cr500 per ton and is sold only at Class A and
              B starports (WBH p. 257); unrefined fuel costs Cr100 per ton and is sold at Class A
              through D starports. A dash in the refined position (tooltip: "unrefined fuel only")
              means the starport doesn't sell refined fuel; a single dash on its own means neither
              grade is sold.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Allegiance</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Political or cultural allegiance code, if assigned.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class=' font-semibold text-highlight mb-2'>Orbital Data</p>
        <dl class='space-y-5 '>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Orbit / AU</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Orbital radius. Shown as orbit number and/or astronomical units depending on campaign display settings. Moon orbits are expressed in planetary diameters.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Period</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Time to complete one orbit around the parent body.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Effective HZCO Deviation</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Deviation from the habitable zone centre orbit, accounting for stellar luminosity and greenhouse effect.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Retrograde</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Whether the body orbits in the direction opposite to the primary star's rotation.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Inclination</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Orbital plane inclination in degrees relative to the stellar equator.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Eccentricity</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Orbital eccentricity (0 = circular; higher values indicate more elliptical orbits).</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Tidally Locked</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>The body to which this object is gravitationally locked, if applicable.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Sidereal Day</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Rotation period relative to the stars, in hours.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Twilight Zone</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Indicates the body orbits within the star's twilight zone, producing extreme day/night temperature differentials.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class=' font-semibold text-highlight mb-2'>Physical Data</p>
        <dl class='space-y-5 '>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Diameter</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Surface diameter in kilometres, with UWP size code.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Mass</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Planetary mass expressed in Earth masses (☉).</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Gravity</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Surface gravity in standard g.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Density</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Mean density expressed relative to Earth (☉).</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class=' font-semibold text-highlight mb-2'>Environmental Data</p>
        <dl class='space-y-5 '>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Temperature</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Mean surface temperature in °C.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Rotation</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Sidereal rotation period in hours.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Axial Tilt</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Axial tilt in degrees; drives seasonal variation.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Albedo</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Fraction of incident solar radiation reflected by the surface and atmosphere (0–1).</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Greenhouse</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Greenhouse effect factor; contributes to surface temperature above the baseline solar input.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Atmosphere</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Atmosphere code with composition type. Tainted atmospheres include irritant, severity, and persistence sub-classifications.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Hydrographics</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Percentage of surface covered by liquid, with liquid type and distribution pattern.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class=' font-semibold text-highlight mb-2'>Biological Data</p>
        <dl class='space-y-5 '>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Habitability Rating</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              A score from 0 to 10 indicating how suitable the world is for unprotected human habitation.
              0 is actively hostile; 10 is a Terra-equivalent garden world. See WBH p. 132.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Biomass Rating</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Total biological mass present on the world, on an arbitrary comparative scale.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Biodiversity Rating</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Number of distinct species or ecological niches. Values of 10 or above are equivalent to pre-human Terra.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Biocomplexity Rating</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Highest level of biological complexity present, from primitive single-cell organisms (1) through extant or extinct sophonts (9) to ecosystem-wide superorganisms (10).</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Resource Rating</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Economic value of extractable resources. Ranges from 2 (none extractable) through 12 (liable to trigger a resource rush).</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class=' font-semibold text-highlight mb-2'>Social Data</p>
        <dl class='space-y-5 '>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Population</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Population code (0–12) with approximate headcount range — or exact census figure, where recorded — concentration rating, urbanisation percentage, and major city count. Where the world's language is known, individual major cities are named and listed by population, highest first.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Major City Type &amp; Capital Designation</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Each major city may be assigned a type, reflecting the minimum tech level required to build it: Arcology,
              sealed city (Ar, TL8); Flying, buoyant gas (Fb, TL8); Flying, grav hover (Fg, TL10); Flying, grav mobile
              (Fm, TL14); Mobile, rails (Mr, TL6); Mobile, tracked (Mt, TL9); Space, spin (Ss, TL8); Space, grav (Sg,
              TL10); Underground, benign environment (Ub, TL6); Underground, hostile environment (Uh, TL8); Water,
              shore floating adjacent (Wa, TL0); Water, static floating deep water (Wd, TL6); Water, free floating
              (Wf, TL8); Water, submerged (Ws, TL9); Water, deep ocean (Wx, TL12). A city left unset is a standard,
              ground-based settlement.
              <br>
              A city may also carry a capital designation: World capital (Cw), Faction capital (Cf), National capital
              (Cn), or Regional capital (Cr). See WBH p. 154.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Native / Extinct Sophont</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Whether a sophont species is or was native to this body.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Government</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Government type code and description. Not shown when population and this value are both 0.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Law Level</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Law level code indicating degree of legal restriction, keyed to weapon restrictions. Not shown when population and this value are both 0.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Tech Level</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Technological sophistication of the dominant culture (0–35). Not shown when population and this value are both 0.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class='pt-2 text-right uppercase font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.22em] text-fg-muted'>
        REFERENCE 0423PB
      </div>
  </div>
<% end %>
